    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-white">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto prose prose-lg">

        <h2 id="what-it-should-do" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">What a driving instructor diary app should actually do</h2>

        <p>
          Every ADI has a diary system that works, mostly. A spiral-bound book in the door pocket, a
          shared Google Calendar, a notes app, a WhatsApp thread per pupil. The trouble is not booking
          a lesson. Anyone can write "Sam, 4pm, Tue". The trouble is everything that has to happen
          around that lesson: checking the car is free, remembering where Sam lives, reminding Sam the
          night before, recording what you covered, and knowing whether Sam has paid.
        </p>

        <p>
          A <strong>driving instructor diary app</strong> earns its place when it does that surrounding
          work for you. When you are comparing ADI diary apps, these are the jobs worth testing on a
          real week of lessons:
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Show the shape of your week.</strong> Day, week and month views, so you can see today's run and next month's gaps without flicking pages.</li>
          <li><strong>Make changes cheap.</strong> Moving a lesson should be a drag, not a rewrite, and the pupil should find out without you sending a separate message.</li>
          <li><strong>Refuse clashes.</strong> Before a booking is saved, check that the pupil, the instructor and the car are all free.</li>
          <li><strong>Keep the pupil next to the lesson.</strong> Address, phone number, notes and progress should be one tap from the slot.</li>
          <li><strong>Chase people for you.</strong> Reminders should go out automatically, and payments should be recorded against the lesson.</li>
          <li><strong>Work wherever you are.</strong> Plan the week on a laptop, run the day from your phone.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>
          DriveSchoolPro was built around that list. It is a full
          <a href="/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">driving school software</a> platform, but
          for most solo instructors the diary is the part they open fifty times a day, so it is the
          part we have spent the most time on. The rest of this page goes through each job in turn,
          including the things it does not do yet.
        </p>

        <h2 id="views" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-12 mb-6">Day, week and month views, with drag-and-drop</h2>

        <p>
          The diary has three views, and each suits a different moment in an instructor's day.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Day view</strong> lists each lesson in order with the time, the pupil and the pick-up address, and marks the gap between lessons so you can see how long you have to get across town.</li>
          <li><strong>Week view</strong> is where most instructors live. It shows every lesson in a time grid, colour-coded, with totals for bookings and cancellations along the top.</li>
          <li><strong>Month view</strong> is a heatmap. Busy weeks look busy and thin weeks look thin, which makes the Tuesday afternoons that never fill impossible to miss.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          To move a lesson, pick it up and drop it in the new slot. The change follows the lesson
          everywhere it appears: your diary, the pupil's own app and the reminder that goes out
          before the lesson. You can also book straight from the calendar with a quick booking panel,
          so there is no separate form to fill in.
        </p>

        <div class="my-8 not-prose">
          <MarketingScreenshot
            src="/images/marketing/calendar-week-view.webp"
            width={1440}
            height={900}
            alt="DriveSchoolPro week view: colour-coded driving lessons in a time grid with booking totals along the top"
          />
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 text-center mt-3">The week view, where most instructors plan from</p>
        </div>

        <p>
          The same diary runs in any web browser and in the DriveSchoolPro apps for iPhone and
          Android. Plan Sunday night's week on a laptop, then run Monday from your phone at the kerb.
          There is nothing to sync by hand, because it is the same diary in every place. More detail
          is on the <a href="/features/scheduling/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">scheduling feature page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="clashes" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-12 mb-6">Clash checks across pupil, instructor and car</h2>

        <p>
          Most double-bookings are not carelessness. They happen because the fact you needed to check
          was somewhere else: in last week's message from the pupil, in another instructor's diary, or
          in your head as "the car's in for its MOT on Thursday". A paper diary cannot check any of
          that, and neither can a general calendar app.
        </p>

        <p>
          DriveSchoolPro checks three things on every booking. Is this pupil already booked? Is this
          instructor already teaching? Is this vehicle already out? If any one of them is taken, the
          booking is stopped before it is saved, and you are told which of the three clashed and when.
          It is the difference between finding out now and finding out at 9am on a Saturday with a
          learner on the doorstep.
        </p>

        <p>
          The vehicle check matters more than people expect. If you share a dual-controlled car with
          a colleague, or keep a spare for when yours is off the road, the diary knows which car each
          lesson is in. The app also tracks each vehicle's MOT, tax and insurance dates and alerts you
          before they lapse, so the car you have booked is a car you can legally teach in.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-brand-navy p-4 my-6 not-prose">
          <p class="font-bold text-brand-navy mb-1">What it doesn't do yet</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700">
            A repeating weekly series for a regular pupil is coming soon; for now each lesson is booked
            individually. There is no two-way sync with Google or Apple Calendar: the diary in
            DriveSchoolPro is the single source, which is what makes the clash checks trustworthy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="pupils" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-12 mb-6">Pupil records, progress and AI lesson briefings</h2>

        <p>
          A diary entry is only useful if you know who you are picking up. In DriveSchoolPro every
          lesson links to the pupil's record: contact details, pick-up address, lesson history,
          notes, remaining lesson balance and progress. Tap the lesson, and it is all there.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-8 mb-4">Progress against the DVSA 27 skills</h3>

        <p>
          Progress is tracked against the official DVSA framework: all
          <a href="/dvsa-27-driving-skills/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">27 driving skills across 8 categories</a>,
          rated on a six-level scale. Logging a lesson takes a couple of taps per skill you worked on,
          so it happens at the kerb before you drive off rather than at eleven at night from memory.
          See <a href="/features/progress-tracking/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">progress tracking</a>
          for how the ratings work.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-8 mb-4">A pupil app of their own</h3>

        <p>
          Each pupil gets their own app on iPhone, Android or the web. They see their upcoming
          lessons, where they stand against each of the 27 skills, and how many hours they have left.
          They can also book from the times you have made available, so the "are you free Thursday?"
          messages dry up. You can <a href="/features/online-booking/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">embed a booking widget</a> on your own website too, so new enquiries
          pick a slot without messaging you first.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-8 mb-4">A briefing before every lesson</h3>

        <p>
          Before each lesson, DriveSchoolPro writes a three- or four-sentence briefing from your last
          notes and the pupil's current skill ratings, using Claude, Anthropic's AI. It is there to
          be read in the ten seconds before you knock on the door, so you start from what happened
          last time rather than from memory. It is particularly useful when another instructor took
          the previous lesson. Read more on the
          <a href="/features/lesson-briefings/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">AI lesson briefings page</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="my-8 not-prose flex justify-center">
          <MarketingScreenshot
            src="/images/marketing/calendar-day-mobile.webp"
            width={640}
            height={1164}
            alt="DriveSchoolPro day view on a phone listing three lessons with pupil names, pick-up addresses and the gaps between them"
            mobile
          />
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 text-center -mt-4 mb-8">The day view on a phone: who, where, and how long until the next one</p>

        <h2 id="payments-reminders" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-12 mb-6">Payments and automatic reminders</h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-8 mb-4">Getting paid from the diary</h3>

        <p>
          Pupils can pay by card through Stripe, from their own app or from a payment link you send.
          The money goes to your own Stripe account. You can sell lesson blocks, say ten hours paid
          up front, which draw down as lessons are taken so both of you can see the remaining balance.
          Cash, bank transfer and cheque can be recorded against a pupil too, so the ledger stays
          right however the money arrived. Card payments taken through DriveSchoolPro carry a
          {PRICING.cardFeePercent}% platform fee plus Stripe's own processing fee. The
          <a href="/features/getting-paid/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">getting paid page</a>
          has the detail.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-8 mb-4">Reminders you don't have to send</h3>

        <p>
          Automatic email reminders go out before every lesson (text and WhatsApp coming soon on
          {PRICING.solo.name} and {PRICING.school.name}), and you choose how far in advance. When
          you drag a lesson to a new time, the reminder moves with it. Email reminders are on every
          plan, including Free. For more on cutting missed lessons, read
          <a href="/blog/reduce-driving-lesson-no-shows/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">how to reduce driving lesson no-shows</a>.
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-brand-red-light">
    <div class="container-custom text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-3">Try the diary on next week's lessons</h2>
