  <section id="paper-vs-app">
    <ComparisonTable
      title="Paper ADI diary vs a general calendar vs DriveSchoolPro"
      description="What each option does for a working driving instructor. A general calendar means Google Calendar, Apple Calendar or similar."
      headers={['DriveSchoolPro', 'Paper diary', 'General calendar app']}
      rows={paperVsApp}
      rowHeader="Job"
      highlightColumn={0}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-white">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto prose prose-lg">

        <p>
          A paper diary is not a bad tool. It never runs out of battery and nobody has to train you
          to use it. Where it falls down is everything joined to the lesson: it cannot remind the
          pupil, check the car, take a payment or show you a month of gaps. A general calendar app
          fixes the battery and the backup, but it does not know what a pupil, a vehicle or a DVSA
          skill is. A dedicated driving lesson diary app is worth it once the admin around your
          lessons starts taking evenings. If you are making that move, our guide to
          <a href="/blog/switch-paper-diary-to-driving-school-software/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">switching from a paper diary</a>
          walks through the first fortnight.
        </p>

        <h2 id="schools" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-12 mb-6">A shared diary for multi-instructor schools</h2>

        <p>
          The same diary scales from one instructor to a school. On the School plan every instructor
          appears in a shared, colour-coded diary, and the clash checks run across all of them. If a
          pupil is already booked with one instructor, another cannot book them into the same slot.
          If two instructors share a car, the diary will not let both take it out at once.
        </p>

        <p>
          That shared view is what makes cover lessons painless. When an instructor is off sick, the
          office can see who has space, move the lesson by dragging it into another instructor's
          column, and the pupil's reminder and app update to match. The covering instructor opens the
          same pupil record, sees the same DVSA progress and gets the same AI briefing, so the pupil
          does not spend ten minutes explaining where they got to. Each instructor can set their own
          working hours and availability, and pupils booking from their app only see times that are
          genuinely free.
        </p>

        <p>
          For the wider picture of running a school, including instructor management and reporting,
          see the <a href="/driving-school-management-software/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">driving school management software guide</a>
          and the <a href="/driving-school-scheduling-software/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">scheduling software guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="pricing" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-12 mb-6">What the diary app costs</h2>

        <p>
          DriveSchoolPro is free for everyone until 31 March 2027 while it is in early access. Every
          feature on this page is included, for solo instructors and schools, and no card is needed
          to start.
        </p>

        <p>{PRICE_LINE}</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>{PRICING.free.name}:</strong> £0 for {PRICING.free.instructors} instructor and up to {PRICING.free.lessonsPerMonth} lessons a month, with every feature including AI lesson briefings.</li>
          <li><strong>{PRICING.solo.name}:</strong> £{PRICING.solo.price} a month for one instructor with unlimited lessons. Text and WhatsApp reminders coming soon.</li>
          <li><strong>{PRICING.school.name}:</strong> £{PRICING.school.price} a month plus £{PRICING.school.perExtraInstructor} for each extra instructor. Text and WhatsApp reminders coming soon.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Instructors who add a card by {PRICING.founding.cardBy} can lock in founding prices for
          {PRICING.founding.lockMonths} months. The full breakdown is on the
          <a href="/pricing/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">pricing page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="choosing" class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mt-12 mb-6">How DriveSchoolPro compares with other ADI diary apps</h2>

        <p>
          The two diary apps UK instructors most often mention are Total Drive and My Drive Time.
          Both are established, both have a pupil app, and both list features DriveSchoolPro does not
          have yet, such as broadcasting a cancelled slot to pupils and free migration of your
          existing records. DriveSchoolPro differs on AI lesson briefings, progress built on the
          DVSA 27 skills, vehicle-aware clash checks and Google review requests when a pupil passes.
        </p>

        <p>
          We have written a neutral
          <a href="/compare/total-drive-vs-mydrivetime/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">Total Drive vs My Drive Time comparison</a>
          that sets the three side by side using each vendor's own website, and there are one-to-one
          pages for <a href="/compare/total-drive/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">DriveSchoolPro vs Total Drive</a>
          and <a href="/compare/my-drive-time/" class="text-brand-red hover:underline">DriveSchoolPro vs My Drive Time</a>.
          The honest advice is the same whichever you pick: run it alongside your current diary for a
          week of real lessons, try to double-book yourself, move a lesson, and see whether the pupil
          hears about it. That week tells you more than any feature list.
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

